gemma: Customer hit a parsing failure using the TypeScript SDK (v1.4.2) in JSON/structured_output streaming mode. They get SyntaxError: Unexpected end of JSON input intermittently. Happening only on Node 18 in our hosted API, looks like the server is splitting an NDJSON object across TCP chunk boundaries. Repro steps below and a small trace attached.

sanjay: can you paste the exact repro and SDK call? node vs browser? http1 chunked or http2?

gemma: Node 18 (fetch + ReadableStream). SDK call is `client.responses.stream({model, input, mode: 'json'})` (converted from our public TS SDK). We read chunks with reader.read() and pass each chunk to the SDK's onProgress JSON parser. Minimal reproduction (pseudo):

```
const r = await fetch(url, opts);
const reader = r.body.getReader();
const decoder = new TextDecoder();
let buf = '';
while(true){
  const {value, done} = await reader.read();
  if(done) break;
  buf += decoder.decode(value, {stream: true});
  // SDK currently tries to parse each event as a standalone JSON
  // sometimes buf contains "{\"a\":1}\n{\"b\":2}\n" but sometimes the boundary splits: "{\"a\":1}\n{\"b":"
}
```

olivia: is the API emitting NDJSON (newline delimited JSON) or SSE events? NDJSON should have a trailing newline for each object. If the server is truncating sends, we should be resilient.

gemma: server is NDJSON. The customer attached raw stream capture where one packet ends with `...}\n{\"b\":` and next packet finishes the rest. SDK's parser tries to parse `{\"b\":` alone and fails. Error trace: `SyntaxError: Unexpected end of JSON input at JSON.parse (<anonymous>)` with stack inside `TSStreamingParser.parseChunk`.

ryan: do we have the request_id or trace id to look at server side? Could be our proxy changing chunking.

gemma: request_id=ri:0a1b2c3d, included the raw bytes in the ticket. It looks like chunking is normal; we see 3 separate TCP segments. The important bit is the client must handle fragmentation.

sanjay: thanks. Immediate workaround for customers: buffer partial data until you detect a newline or until braces are balanced. Example quick buff approach:```
let pending = '';
for await (const chunk of stream) {
  pending += chunk;
  let idx;
  while ((idx = pending.indexOf('\n')) !== -1) {
    const line = pending.slice(0, idx).trim();
    pending = pending.slice(idx + 1);
    if (!line) continue;
    try { const obj = JSON.parse(line); onMessage(obj); } catch(e){ /* ignore until more data */ }
  }
}
```

olivia: long-term we should make SDK resilient by default. Current SDK v1.4.2 assumes each progress callback gets a full JSON object. That's brittle. We need to accept partial chunks and assemble until newline, or better yet use a small NDJSON tokenizer.

sanjay: agree. I'm going to prepare a fix in the parser: accumulate raw text, loop over newline boundaries, and only JSON.parse complete lines. If no newline policy (some servers stream objects without newlines) fallback to brace-balancing up to a reasonable size. I'll open a PR.

ci-bot: build/PR #4789 created by @sanjay — smoke tests queued :ci_pipeline: 

sanjay: PR #4789 includes: - incremental buffer in stream parser - newline-first tokenization - fallback balanced-braces for non-newline NDJSON - unit tests simulating chunk splits and multibyte utf8 boundary splits. Patch will be in v1.4.3-beta.

gemma: can you include a small repro in the PR so I can point the customer to it? /cc @olivia for test coverage.

sanjay: included a repro harness (node script that forcibly slices the stream mid-object). also added an integration that runs the harness in CI.

olivia: also add a short note in docs: "For streaming JSON/structured output, prefer newline-delimited JSON (NDJSON). The SDK will buffer partial chunks but NDJSON is the most robust and efficient."

gemma: relaying to customer now. They asked about ETA for patch/semver.

sanjay: aiming for beta within 24 hours, stable release in next minor if no regressions. I'll post the 1.4.3-beta tarball link here once available.

(10h later)
gemma: customer tested 1.4.3-beta and confirmed it resolves the intermittent parse errors. :tada: They said latency was unchanged. Closing for now; follow-up is to add docs note and postmortem in eng-docs if we see more incidents.
